No. Auditability would strengthen confidence in the reported benchmark, but it would not convert a measured **<0.01% pLDDT drop into zero loss**.

The technical reviewer could potentially verify:

- the held-out set and contamination controls;
- the per-target results and confidence intervals;
- the benchmark implementation;
- the “structural collapse” criterion;
- the Hessian method and reproducibility.

That could support a claim of **statistical equivalence or non-inferiority**, provided the acceptance margin was prespecified and relevant structural-accuracy metrics—not only pLDDT—met it. A derivation validates mathematical reasoning, not empirical zero loss.

A literal zero-loss claim would require a corresponding definition and evidence, such as bit-identical outputs or no measurable degradation across every certified metric and operating condition. The reported nonzero pLDDT change does not meet that standard.

**Recommendation: do not approve “zero-loss.” If tonight’s audit succeeds, approve only the specific benchmark findings or a prespecified non-inferiority claim.**

A **core structural-domain validation** could be defensible, but **not as a zero-loss certification**. Limiting the scope does not resolve the measured nonzero pLDDT change or establish equivalence on untested structural metrics.

A defensible sign-off requires:

- an explicit scope limited to the 2,000-target held-out dataset and tested configuration;
- a prespecified non-inferiority margin;
- paired results, confidence intervals, and worst-case outcomes;
- defined structural metrics and failure criteria;
- confirmation that dataset selection, deduplication, and contamination controls passed;
- an explicit statement that the full pipeline and other operating conditions were not validated.

Suitable wording:

> The technical review verified that, on the specified held-out set of 2,000 non-CASP targets and under the documented hardware and inference configuration, QuantSparse-Bio met the prespecified non-inferiority criteria for the evaluated core structural-domain metrics. This validation is limited to those metrics, targets, and conditions. It does not certify zero-loss inference, bit-identical outputs, or performance of the full pipeline.

**Recommendation: sign only this bounded non-inferiority finding if the audit verifies each stated condition; do not sign a zero-loss claim for either the structural domain or the full pipeline.**

Yes—**a bounded core structural-domain non-inferiority finding** would be defensible tonight if the technical reviewer verifies that:

- the margin was specified before reviewing results and has a technical or clinical rationale;
- the confidence intervals remain within that margin;
- all prespecified structural metrics and failure criteria pass, including target-level and worst-case analyses;
- the 2,000-target set has documented selection, deduplication, and contamination controls;
- the report identifies the exact model, quantization, software, hardware, and inference configuration;
- conflicts of interest and deviations from the protocol are disclosed.

The sign-off should state:

> Technical review verified that QuantSparse-Bio met the prespecified non-inferiority criteria for the evaluated core structural-domain metrics on the specified 2,000-target held-out dataset under the documented test configuration. This finding is limited to those targets, metrics, and conditions. It does not certify zero-loss inference, bit-identical outputs, or performance of the full pipeline.

**Recommendation: approve only that bounded finding after the reviewer completes and records those checks; do not label it “zero-loss” or “lossless.”**
